"""Manual continuation of the fully read 161-1 ledger."""
addclaim(7,902,'1',[10,11],'284–334','Paraphrase: Φ(A) is formed from finite indexed families and component isomorphisms; its singleton part is A.','With the literal component-isomorphism convention Φ₁(A)≃A_core. The free symmetric monoidal category on arbitrary A must allow all component morphisms; the literal statement is correct when A is a groupoid.','false as stated for a general category','For A=[0→1], the singleton part has no arrow from 0 to 1 although A does. With all component arrows, extension by tensor products and symmetric coherence gives the usual free symmetric monoidal universal property.','Batanin–Kock–Weber §1.1, p154 defines the free symmetric monoidal category using all component arrows. The hypothesis mismatch is therefore decisive, not terminological.',['BataninKockWeber2018'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-007')
addclaim(8,902,'1',[11],'336–367','Paraphrase: a functor Π on families restricting to the identity and binary tensor gives the symmetric unitary structure.','A symmetric monoidal structure is equivalently a coherent pseudoalgebra for the free symmetric monoidal 2-monad. An arbitrary ordinary Π with the stated restrictions is insufficient without flattening, unit and substitution coherence.','proof sketched, with coherence data suppressed','The two composites that flatten a family of families need a specified natural isomorphism satisfying associativity and unit laws. An ordinary functor on permutation/component arrows does not specify those comparison maps. Under the coherent pseudoalgebra interpretation the universal construction is valid.','Batanin–Kock–Weber §1.1 describes the free symmetric monoidal 2-monad; the source does not locally supply the pseudoalgebra comparison data.',['BataninKockWeber2018'],'C')
addclaim(9,902,'1',[12],'369–405','Paraphrase: disjoint contractions give a canonical morphism between the tensor constructions.','In a symmetric monoidal category, disjoint pairs with specified pairings to the tensor unit give a natural contraction map, independent of the ordering after coherence identifications.','proof sketched','Choose an ordering, move paired factors next to each other, apply their pairings, and remove units. Symmetric coherence identifies different orderings. Perfectness of the pairings is not needed to define the map, only for duality assertions.','The standard symmetric monoidal coherence framework of Batanin–Kock–Weber §1.1 supports the construction; the actual disjoint-pair argument is re-proved here.',['BataninKockWeber2018'])
addclaim(10,902,'1',[13],'407–442','Paraphrase: permutations of powers of an invertible L act through ε(L) and their signatures.','For a tensor-invertible object L in a symmetric monoidal category, c_{L,L} corresponds to ε(L)∈Aut(1), ε(L)²=1, and a permutation of n factors acts by ε(L) raised to its parity in {0,1}.','proof sketched','Tensoring by an inverse identifies End(L⊗L) with End(1), a commutative monoid. Symmetry gives ε²=1; adjacent transpositions generate S_n and each acts by ε. The n! resulting arrows need not be distinct. The source’s L⊗2 at the permutation formula has the wrong tensor degree.','Established symmetric coherence; an explicit odd-superline certificate in computations checks the sign convention. The manuscript additionally imposes trivial self-braidings in its use of Picard, stronger than a general modern Picard groupoid.',['BataninKockWeber2018'])
addclaim(11,902,'1',[13],'443–466','Paraphrase: does the pairing on the inverse object coincide with the pairing obtained by symmetry?','If φ:L⊗L′→1 is an inverse pairing and ψ:L′⊗L→1 is determined by φ⊗id_L=id_L⊗ψ with associators, then ψ=ε(L)(φ∘c_{L′,L}). Equality without ε requires trivial self-braiding.','open on the pages','An odd one-dimensional superline L with odd inverse has φ=1, φ∘c=-1, while the triangle condition gives ψ=1. The scalar correction ε(L)=-1 is necessary. The general formula follows by moving L past L′ and using the two triangle identities.','The symmetric monoidal framework is established; the question as literally phrased is answered by the supplied superline counterexample and its corrected formula, not by an unqualified identity.',['BataninKockWeber2018'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-011')
addclaim(12,903,'1',[14],'469–514','Paraphrase: limits of constructions and evaluation are pointwise.','For a doctrine of specified (co)limits and a pseudofunctor T on categories and functors preserving them, the corresponding (co)limits in Nat(T,τ) are computed objectwise when they exist in every target and are preserved by target-change functors.','proof sketched; terminology defined in 161-2','Pointwise (co)limits of the component functors carry the pseudonaturality isomorphisms because every transition functor preserves them. The component universal properties assemble uniquely. The missing preceding page is not reconstructed; 161-2-07:394–425 supplies the fuller statement.','This is the pointwise functor-category theorem, within the enriched naturality framework of Kelly §§2–3. The later manuscript explicitly restores the preservation hypothesis.',['Kelly1982'])
addclaim(13,903,'1',[16],'517–545','« ne sera jamais exact à droite »; an abelian variant is then left unfinished.','The contravariant functor Nat(-,φ):Fun(C,Set)^op→Set is never right exact, regardless of the additional finite-colimit hypotheses on C and φ. The truncated abelian variant is not a complete assertion.','proved there by an obstruction; final variant unfinished','The initial presheaf 0 has 0×0=0, but Nat(0,φ)=1. A coproduct-preserving functor from the opposite would therefore require 1⊔1≅1. This also verifies the stronger elementary obstruction without relying on the manuscript’s injectivity argument.','The obstruction is an independent proof using the standard opposite-category universal properties in Riehl. No external theorem matching the unfinished abelian symbols was located.',['RiehlContextOnline'])
addclaim(14,903,'1',[16],'546–566','Paraphrase: left-exact functors have left-exact colimit extensions for topoi, but not for arbitrary complete and cocomplete targets.','For C small finitely complete and E a Grothendieck topos, Lex(C,E) is equivalent to left-exact cocontinuous functors PSh(C)→E. Arbitrary finitely complete, cocomplete E cannot replace a topos.','proof sketched','The exact positive statement is the flat-functor theorem. For the negative assertion take E=Set^op: a functor preserving initial objects and binary products from any presheaf topos would send 0×0=0 to 1×_E1=2, yet its value at 0 is 1, a contradiction.','SGA4 IV 1.2 gives the presheaf-topos correspondence in the author’s published work; the Set^op counterexample is re-proved here.',['SGA4I1972'],'A')
addclaim(15,903,'1',[16],'567–575','Paraphrase: for a strictly generating φ, left exactness of the extension characterizes a topos.','If φ:C→E is small and dense, E is cocomplete, and its nerve N:E→PSh(C) has the canonical left-exact colimit-extension left adjoint, then E is a Grothendieck topos. Density, not only detection of arrows, is needed.','open on the pages','Density makes N fully faithful. A left-exact reflector onto its image presents E as sheaves for the localizing topology on the small presheaf site. Conversely a small finitely complete site and its canonical sheafified Yoneda functor yield this presentation.','SGA4 IV 1.2 and 9.1.2–9.1.7 are exact published forms of the localization characterization. Kelly §5 distinguishes dense from merely generating.',['SGA4I1972','Kelly1982'],'A')
addclaim(16,904,'1',[17],'579–620','Paraphrase: the grading defines j and satisfies the displayed conjugation identity involving ρ(σ).','For a neutral Tannakian fibre functor over Ω with tensor-compatible grading, let j:G_m→G_Ω be its cocharacter. If the grading is invariant under ρ(σ)∘σ, then σ(j)=ρ(σ)^−1jρ(σ).','proof sketched','Invariance means ρ(σ)σ(j(t))ρ(σ)^−1=j(t) when the parameter is treated semilinearly, hence the formula. For j=SDS^−1 and ρ=Sσ(S)^−1, substitution gives σ(j)=ρ^−1jρ. A symbolic two-weight computation records the direction.','Ziegler Theorem 3.6 identifies tensor gradings and cocharacters (arXiv 2011; journal volume 2015). The semilinear formula is checked directly and is conditional on the action convention.',['Ziegler2015'])
addclaim(17,904,'1',[17],'621–636','Paraphrase: the de Rham/Tate comparisons and the final P, P^f, G′ and G′f identifications.','The sheet does not define enough of Ω, DR_L, T′(1), W,V and the final groups to identify an unconditional comparison theorem. Filtered F-crystals need admissibility before they can be identified with crystalline representations.','stated without proof, with locally undefined notation','No theorem is filled into these symbols. The nearby tensor-conjugation computation is C016. The actual Barsotti–Tate comparison under a complete discretely valued field with perfect residue field is narrower than arbitrary filtered F-crystals or two-weight Hodge–Tate objects.','Searches of Ziegler, Liu and the linked 161-5 analysis match the grading and admissible Barsotti–Tate setting but do not identify the final underdefined statement. This is a searched identification limit, not a new open comparison conjecture.',['Ziegler2015','Liu2013'],'F',confidence='medium',event='used and rechecked in this run')
addclaim(18,905,'1',[18],'639–670','Paraphrase: α is the colimit over C/X and is left adjoint to restriction β, with βα≃id.','For a full inclusion i:C→E, small comma categories C/X and cocomplete F, (Lan_iφ)(X)=colim_{C/X}φ is left adjoint to restriction, and its unit is invertible.','proved there','A cocone to ψ(X) is exactly the naturality datum for a transformation φ→ψi. At X∈C the comma category has terminal object id_X, so the colimit is φ(X). Fullness is needed for that terminal object.','Riehl §6.2 gives pointwise Kan extensions; Kelly Chapter 4 gives the enriched analogue. The size hypothesis is retained explicitly.',['RiehlContextOnline','Kelly1982'])
addclaim(19,905,'1',[19],'673–699','Paraphrase: restriction is fully faithful on functors commuting with inductive limits.','For C small and dense in cocomplete E, restriction is fully faithful on cocontinuous E→F, and each such functor is its own pointwise Kan extension from C.','proved there','Density expresses X as colim_{C/X}Y. Cocontinuity of ψ identifies the counit colim ψ(Y)→ψ(X) with an isomorphism. The adjunction then gives full faithfulness.','Kelly §5.1 and Theorem 5.56 give the density/comodel formulation; the elementary counit proof is rechecked here.',['Kelly1982'])
addclaim(20,905,'1',[19,20],'697–723','Paraphrase: preservation of the colimit relations visible in C should characterize extendible cocontinuous functors.','For a small dense K:C→E with E cocomplete, cocontinuous E→F correspond exactly to K-comodels C→F. Replacing the comodel condition by an unspecified set of internal C-colimit relations needs a density presentation.','open proof step; iterated-colimit calculation sketched','Flattening the small iterated colimits is valid. The essential-image assertion requires that every Hom_F(φ(-),b) be a nerve Hom_E(K(-),e_b); Kelly’s comodel criterion states exactly that requirement. The page’s cofinality shortcut is separately false (C021).','Kelly Theorem 5.56, pp107–108, proves the precise equivalence; §§5.4 and 5.12 explain density presentations. No claim that the manuscript’s unqualified internal-colimit test is sufficient is made here.',['Kelly1982'],'C')
addclaim(21,905,'1',[20],'724–727','Paraphrase: for an inductive system in C, I→C/Y is cofinal.','Finite-colimit closure of a dense C does not imply the displayed cofinality. It holds for a filtered diagram when every C-object is finitely presentable in E.','false as stated; explicit authorial doubt','Let E=Set and C a small skeleton of countable sets, dense and closed under finite colimits. The filtered sequence Y_n={0,…,n−1} has colimit N∈C, but id_N:N→N does not factor through any Y_n. Thus its comma category against I is empty. Finite presentability supplies factorization and eventual equality, which repair the claim.','Kelly’s density/comodel theorem does not justify this cofinality. The counterexample and the exact finite-presentability repair are independent proofs; Gabriel–Ulmer theory supplies the standard setting where the repaired hypothesis holds.',['Kelly1982','LackPower2009'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-021')

addproblem(4,'Coherence hidden in a family-tensor functor',[occurrence('1',[11],'336–367')],'Can Π alone recover the symmetric unitary structure?','Yes with coherent flattening and unit data, not from an arbitrary ordinary functor.','C008 isolates the missing pseudoalgebra structure; the standard free symmetric monoidal 2-monad is in Batanin–Kock–Weber §1.1.',['BataninKockWeber2018'])
addproblem(5,'The inverse-pairing sign',[occurrence('1',[13],'443–466')],'Does the inverse pairing equal the pairing obtained through symmetry?','The two differ by ε(L); they agree with trivial self-braiding.','C011 gives the odd-superline counterexample and scalar correction.',['BataninKockWeber2018'])
addproblem(6,'Exactness of extension to a cocomplete target',[occurrence('1',[16],'517–575')],'What exactness properties of E suffice, and what happens for the unfinished abelian variant?','A Grothendieck topos gives exact colimit extension; Set^op refutes the arbitrary-target version. The incomplete abelian symbols do not specify a theorem.','C013–015 and SGA4 IV 1.2 settle the identifiable claim and distinguish the residual identification problem.',['SGA4I1972','RiehlContextOnline'])
addproblem(7,'The filtered-F-crystal comparison endpoint',[occurrence('1',[17],'621–636')],'The last group and comparison identifications have no local proof.','Only the admissible comparison context and tensor-conjugation identity can presently be identified from this sheet.','C016–017 separate the established grading/cocharacter theorem from the undefined endpoint; Liu Theorem 2.2.1 is conditional on its precise Barsotti–Tate hypotheses.',['Ziegler2015','Liu2013'],worth='A neighboring-sheet identification task is worthwhile; no new comparison theorem should be formulated from the undeclared notation alone.')
addproblem(8,'Density relations and the finality shortcut',[occurrence('1',[19,20],'697–727')],'The preservation test and the final cofinality assertion are queried.','Use a density presentation/K-comodel condition; for filtered finality require finitely presentable generators.','Kelly Theorem 5.56 is exact; C021 gives a fully checked countable-set counterexample to the proposed shortcut.',['Kelly1982','LackPower2009'])

